CREATE DATABASE studentdb;

	1. BIT - BIT(n):
	----------------
		1. BIT is used to store the bits values, which is basically 0's & 1's. It can store upto 64bits.
		2. Syntax: b'1101' or B'1101 (way1)
				   0b101001 (way 2)
		3. By default value of n is 1
		4. if bit(n) where n is set to 7 bits, then if you try to add 8bits value with leading zero, it will pad 0's and insert 
			value w/o error. 
		5. use bin(column_name) to show binary value - Ex: select bin(studentID) from studentInfo;
		6. lpad function is used to retain the leading 0's - Ex: select lpad(bin(column_name), bit_size, '0')) from studentInfo;
		
	2. BOOLEAN / BOOL / TINYINT:
	----------------------------
	MySQL does not have built-in Boolean type. However, it uses TINYINT(1) instead. To make it more convenient, 
	MySQL provides BOOLEAN or BOOL as the synonym of TINYINT(1).

	In MySQL, zero is considered as false, and non-zero value is considered as true. To use Boolean literals, 
	you use the constants TRUE and FALSE that evaluate to 1 and 0 respectively. 
	
	If you use the following statement, which will the give result values where we have boolean value as 1.
	i.e., select * from studentInfo where attendance = 1 or true;
	but all non-zero values are also considered as zero, so if we have set any column with non-zero value, if 
	you want to retrive them , then use the following:
	
	is true, is not true
	is fals, is not false
	
	If you want to output the result as true and false, you can use the IF function as follows:
	Example: select Name, if(attendance, 'true', 'false') from studentInfo;
	
	3. CHAR:
	--------
	When you query the CHAR value, MySQL removes the trailing spaces. However, while querying the trailing spaces are considered.
	
	Note that MySQL will not remove the trailing spaces if you enable the PAD_CHAR_TO_FULL_LENGTH SQL mode.
	
	LENGTH function is used to get the length of the character. Example: select location, length(location) from studentInfo;
	
	4. DATE:
	--------
	 MySQL uses yyyy-mm-dd format for storing a date value. This format is fixed and it is not possible to change it.
	 
	 To get the current date and time, you use NOW() function. Example: SELECT NOW();
	 To get only date part of a DATETIME value, you use the DATE() function. Example: SELECT DATE(NOW());
	 To format a date value, you use  DATE_FORMAT function. Example: SELECT DATE_FORMAT(CURDATE(), '%m/%d/%Y') today;
	 To get the current system date, you use  CURDATE() function as follows: select CURDATE();
	 To calculate the number of days between two date values, you use the DATEDIFF function: SELECT DATEDIFF('2015-11-04','2014-11-04') days;
	 
	 To add/sub date: DATE_ADD, DATE_SUB
	SELECT '2015-01-01' start,
    DATE_ADD('2015-01-01', INTERVAL 1 DAY) 'one day later',
    DATE_ADD('2015-01-01', INTERVAL 1 WEEK) 'one week later',
    DATE_ADD('2015-01-01', INTERVAL 1 MONTH) 'one month later',
    DATE_ADD('2015-01-01', INTERVAL 1 YEAR) 'one year later';
	
